issue_context.body:
When using runtime_mappings in a datafeed and executing a cross cluster search that hits an older cluster, the datafeed only writes out an audit message indicating the search failed.

It would be better to prevent the datafeed from being started at all. Indicating that one or more of the remote clusters don't support runtime_mappings.

Other things like new aggregations, etc. are more complicated. But, since runtime_mappings are a new field in the datafeed, it would be nice to indicate if they aren't supported by all the clusters in the source indices.

Example:

```
PUT _ml/anomaly_detectors/flight-count
{
  "description" : "count",
  "analysis_config" : {
    "bucket_span":"15m",
    "detectors": [
      {
        "detector_description": "count",
        "by_field_name": "day_of_week",
        "function": "count"
      }
    ]
  },
  "data_description" : {
    "time_field":"timestamp",
    "time_format": "epoch_ms"
  }
}
```

```
PUT _ml/datafeeds/datafeed-flight-count
{
  "job_id": "flight-count",
  "indices": ["cluster_zero:kibana*"],
  "runtime_mappings": {
  "day_of_week": {
    "type": "keyword",
    "script": {
      "source": "emit(doc['timestamp'].value.dayOfWeekEnum.getDisplayName(TextStyle.FULL, Locale.ROOT))"
    }
  }
}
}
```

This results in an audit message:
```
"message": "Datafeed is encountering errors extracting data: Versions before 7.11.0 don't support [runtime_mappings] and search was sent to [7.9.4]",
                    "level": "error",
```

It would be nice to prevent starting the datafeed altogether.
